{"sourceSha256": "8f96adc70404f18b7dae9d47ec451c0d17f40b1d15a82ac1f7e268348476ae88", "text": "OET SAMPLE TEST\n\nROLEPLAYER CARD NO. 4 MEDICINE\n\nSETTING Emergency Department\n\nPARENT You are the parent of a four-year-old boy who came to the Emergency Department\ntwo hours ago, after 36 hours of recurrent vomiting and stomach pain. The doctor\n\ntold you that your son had viral gastroenteritis. He was kept in for two hours on oral\nre-hydration fluids. Your son is not present for your discussion with the doctor.\n\nTASK e When asked, say you still don’t really understand what viral gastroenteritis is.\ne Say you don't know how your son got viral gastroenteritis.\ne Say your son looks very weak; you really think he needs to be kept in hospital.\n\ne When asked, say you're concerned about taking your son home; you just don’t\nknow what to do if he starts to feel worse at home.\n\ne Say you feel better about taking your son home now that you know what to look\nfor and when to come back to the Emergency Department.\n\n| © Cambridge Boxhill Language Assessment SAMPLE TEST\n\nOET SAMPLE TEST\n\nCANDIDATE CARD NO. 4 MEDICINE\n\nSETTING Emergency Department\n\nDOCTOR The parent presented two hours ago at the Emergency Department with his/her\nfour-year-old son. The child had a 36-hour history of recurrent vomiting and\nstomach pain which was diagnosed as viral gastroenteritis. He was given oral\nre-hydration fluids and observed for two hours. He is now ready to be discharged.\nThe child is not present for your discussion with the parent.\n\nTASK © Confirm child is ready to be discharged. Find out about parent's concerns.\n\n¢ Explain viral gastroenteritis (€.g., irritation of stomach or intestines due to viral\ninfection, etc.).\n\n¢ Give information about how gastroenteritis is spread (e.g., contact with\nvomit/faeces of infected person: shaking hands, contaminated foods/objects,\netc.).\n\n© Resist request to keep child in hospital (e.g., oral re-hydration therapy usually\neffective, etc.). Advise on hydration and appropriate clear fluids (e.g., watered\ndown unsweetened fruit juice, electrolyte drinks, etc.). Find out any other\nconcems,\n\ne Advise when to seek medical advice (e.g., signs of severe dehydration: extreme\nthirst, lethargy, irritability, pale/sunken eyes, etc.).\n\n| © Cambridge Boxhill Language Assessment SAMPLE TEST", "pages": [{"number": 1, "text": "OET SAMPLE TEST\n\nROLEPLAYER CARD NO. 4 MEDICINE\n\nSETTING Emergency Department\n\nPARENT You are the parent of a four-year-old boy who came to the Emergency Department\ntwo hours ago, after 36 hours of recurrent vomiting and stomach pain. The doctor\n\ntold you that your son had viral gastroenteritis. He was kept in for two hours on oral\nre-hydration fluids. Your son is not present for your discussion with the doctor.\n\nTASK e When asked, say you still don’t really understand what viral gastroenteritis is.\ne Say you don't know how your son got viral gastroenteritis.\ne Say your son looks very weak; you really think he needs to be kept in hospital.\n\ne When asked, say you're concerned about taking your son home; you just don’t\nknow what to do if he starts to feel worse at home.\n\ne Say you feel better about taking your son home now that you know what to look\nfor and when to come back to the Emergency Department.\n\n| © Cambridge Boxhill Language Assessment SAMPLE TEST\n\nOET SAMPLE TEST\n\nCANDIDATE CARD NO. 4 MEDICINE\n\nSETTING Emergency Department\n\nDOCTOR The parent presented two hours ago at the Emergency Department with his/her\nfour-year-old son. The child had a 36-hour history of recurrent vomiting and\nstomach pain which was diagnosed as viral gastroenteritis. He was given oral\nre-hydration fluids and observed for two hours. He is now ready to be discharged.\nThe child is not present for your discussion with the parent.\n\nTASK © Confirm child is ready to be discharged. Find out about parent's concerns.\n\n¢ Explain viral gastroenteritis (€.g., irritation of stomach or intestines due to viral\ninfection, etc.).\n\n¢ Give information about how gastroenteritis is spread (e.g., contact with\nvomit/faeces of infected person: shaking hands, contaminated foods/objects,\netc.).\n\n© Resist request to keep child in hospital (e.g., oral re-hydration therapy usually\neffective, etc.). Advise on hydration and appropriate clear fluids (e.g., watered\ndown unsweetened fruit juice, electrolyte drinks, etc.). Find out any other\nconcems,\n\ne Advise when to seek medical advice (e.g., signs of severe dehydration: extreme\nthirst, lethargy, irritability, pale/sunken eyes, etc.).\n\n| © Cambridge Boxhill Language Assessment SAMPLE TEST", "extraction": "ocr", "layout": {"version": 1, "width": 1964.0, "height": 2800.0, "blocks": [{"text": "OET SAMPLE TEST", "lines": [{"text": "OET SAMPLE TEST", "x": 0.40988, "y": 0.02429, "width": 0.22149, "height": 0.01393, "confidence": 0.949, "words": [{"text": "OET", "x": 0.40988, "width": 0.04786, "confidence": 0.918}, {"text": "SAMPLE", "x": 0.46792, "width": 0.09674, "confidence": 0.964}, {"text": "TEST", "x": 0.57128, "width": 0.06008, "confidence": 0.963}], "uncertainWords": []}]}, {"text": "ROLEPLAYER CARD NO. 4 MEDICINE", "lines": [{"text": "ROLEPLAYER CARD NO. 4 MEDICINE", "x": 0.03411, "y": 0.05, "width": 0.72556, "height": 0.0125, "confidence": 0.95, "words": [{"text": "ROLEPLAYER", "x": 0.03411, "width": 0.15835, "confidence": 0.909}, {"text": "CARD", "x": 0.20163, "width": 0.06568, "confidence": 0.963}, {"text": "NO.", "x": 0.27902, "width": 0.03819, "confidence": 0.957}, {"text": "4", "x": 0.32637, "width": 0.0112, "confidence": 0.957}, {"text": "MEDICINE", "x": 0.64766, "width": 0.11202, "confidence": 0.962}], "uncertainWords": []}]}, {"text": "SETTING Emergency Department", "lines": [{"text": "SETTING Emergency Department", "x": 0.03259, "y": 0.085, "width": 0.40937, "height": 0.01429, "confidence": 0.962, "words": [{"text": "SETTING", "x": 0.03259, "width": 0.07994, "confide